        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "a97206fe-f4f2-5bd4-83eb-1c8e6927258c",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Jack Young, Arthur Payne\n- Events: 1952 Individual Speedway World Championship, London Riders' Championship\n- Topics: speedway, Australian championships\n\nFacts:\n- In 1952, Jack Young won his second straight Speedway World Championship with 14 points at the 1952 Individual Speedway World Championship.\n- In 1952, Jack Young became the first rider to win the Speedway World Championship twice and the first rider to win it in consecutive years.\n- In 1952, Arthur Payne finished fifth in the Speedway World Championship final.\n- In 1953, Jack Young won the London Riders' Championship.\n- In 1954, Jack Young won the London Riders' Championship again.\n- Between 1948 and 1964, Jack Young won the South Australian Championship nine times.\n- Arthur Payne was born on 28 August 1923 in Sydney, New South Wales.\n- Arthur Payne was an Australian international speedway rider.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "633e9ae5-391b-5fa0-9e6a-16673090ae54",
        "text": "Page: 1952 Individual Speedway World Championship. Sentence: Australian rider Jack Young became the first ever dual winner (and the first to win two in a row) when he won his second straight World Championship after scoring 14 points.\nPage: Jack Young (speedway rider). Sentence: He also won the London Riders' Championship 1953 and 1954 and was a nine time South Australian Champion between 1948 and 1964.\nPage: Arthur Payne (speedway rider). Sentence: Arthur Payne (born 28 August 1923 in Sydney, New South Wales) was an Australian international speedway rider who finished fifth in the 1952 Speedway World Championship final.",
